---
import { renderToString } from "katex";
import BengioPrerequisite from "./BengioPrerequisite.astro";
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import JourneyMap from "./JourneyMap.astro";
import ModeSwitch from "./ModeSwitch.astro";
import WordPredictionLedger from "./WordPredictionLedger.astro";
import { getJourneyOverview, type StageView } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { view: StageView }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const { mode } = view;
const expert = mode === "expert";
const journey = getJourneyOverview("recurrent-memory");
const prerequisite = view.interludes.find((item) => item.id === "bengio-fixed-window-prerequisite");
if (prerequisite === undefined) throw new Error("Mikolov is missing its fixed-window prerequisite.");
const claim = (suffix: string) => {
  const evidence = view.evidence.find((item) => item.claimId === `mikolov-2010-${suffix}`);
  if (evidence === undefined) throw new Error(`Missing recurrent word-model evidence: ${suffix}`);
  return evidence;
};
const math = (tex: string) => renderToString(tex, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
const input = math(String.raw`x(t)=w(t)+s(t-1)\quad\text{(printed equation 1)}`);
const hidden = math(String.raw`\begin{aligned}s_j(t)&=f\!\left(\sum_i x_i(t)u_{ji}\right)\\f(z)&=\frac{1}{1+e^{-z}}\end{aligned}`);
const output = math(String.raw`\begin{aligned}y_k(t)&=g\!\left(\sum_j s_j(t)v_{kj}\right)\\g(z_m)&=\frac{e^{z_m}}{\sum_k e^{z_k}}\end{aligned}`);
const error = math(String.raw`\operatorname{error}(t)=\operatorname{desired}(t)-y(t)`);
const rare = math(String.raw`P(w_i(t+1)\mid w(t),s(t-1))=\begin{cases}y_{\mathrm{rare}}(t)/C_{\mathrm{rare}}&\text{if }w_i\text{ is rare}\\y_i(t)&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}`);
const scaling = [
  { data: "200,000", model: "90/2", knPpl: 336, mixPpl: 271, knWer: "16.4%", mixWer: "15.4%" },
  { data: "1 million", model: "90/2", knPpl: 287, mixPpl: 225, knWer: "15.1%", mixWer: "14.0%" },
  { data: "6.4 million", model: "250/5", knPpl: 221, mixPpl: 156, knWer: "13.5%", mixWer: "11.7%" },
];
const ensemble = [
  { model: "KN5 baseline", rnnPpl: "—", mixPpl: 221, rnnWer: "—", mixWer: "13.5%" },
  { model: "RNN 60/20", rnnPpl: 229, mixPpl: 186, rnnWer: "13.2%", mixWer: "12.6%" },
  { model: "RNN 90/10", rnnPpl: 202, mixPpl: 173, rnnWer: "12.8%", mixWer: "12.2%" },
  { model: "RNN 250/5", rnnPpl: 173, mixPpl: 155, rnnWer: "12.3%", mixWer: "11.7%" },
  { model: "RNN 250/2", rnnPpl: 176, mixPpl: 156, rnnWer: "12.0%", mixWer: "11.9%" },
  { model: "RNN 400/10", rnnPpl: 171, mixPpl: 152, rnnWer: "12.5%", mixWer: "12.1%" },
  { model: "Three RNNs · static", rnnPpl: 151, mixPpl: 143, rnnWer: "11.6%", mixWer: "11.3%" },
  { model: "Three RNNs · dynamic", rnnPpl: 128, mixPpl: 121, rnnWer: "11.3%", mixWer: "11.1%" },
];
const crossData = [
  { model: "Lattice 1-best", dev: "12.9%", eval: "18.4%" },
  { model: "KN5 · 37M", dev: "12.2%", eval: "17.2%" },
  { model: "Discriminative LM [8] · 37M", dev: "11.5%", eval: "16.9%" },
  { model: "Joint LM [9] · 70M", dev: "—", eval: "16.7%" },
  { model: "Three static RNNs + KN5 · 37M", dev: "11.0%", eval: "15.5%" },
  { model: "Three dynamic RNNs + KN5 · 37M", dev: "10.7%", eval: "16.3%", footnote: true },
];
const rt05 = [
  { model: "RT05 LM", static: "24.5%", dynamic: "—" },
  { model: "RT09 LM baseline", static: "24.1%", dynamic: "—" },
  { model: "KN5 · in-domain", static: "25.7%", dynamic: "—" },
  { model: "RNN 500/10 · in-domain", static: "24.2%", dynamic: "24.1%" },
  { model: "RNN 500/10 + RT09", static: "23.3%", dynamic: "23.2%" },
  { model: "RNN 800/10 · in-domain", static: "24.3%", dynamic: "23.8%" },
  { model: "RNN 800/10 + RT09", static: "23.4%", dynamic: "23.1%" },
  { model: "RNN 1000/5 · in-domain", static: "24.2%", dynamic: "23.7%" },
  { model: "RNN 1000/5 + RT09", static: "23.4%", dynamic: "22.9%" },
  { model: "Three RNNs + RT09", static: "23.3%", dynamic: "22.8%" },
];
---

<article class="stage-shell word-model-stage">
  <div class="stage-reading">
    <ModeSwitch activeMode={mode} alternateHref={view.alternateMode.href} />
    <header class="stage-header">
      <p class="stage-number">Stage 5 of 6 · Interspeech · September 2010</p>
      <h1>{expert ? "Where does recurrent word prediction stop learning backward?" : "How does carried state become a word prediction?"}</h1>
      <p class="stage-deck">{expert ? "Reconstruct the normalized Elman word model, keep its depth-one training boundary beside forward recurrence, and audit what each reported system actually combines." : "A changing state can help predict the next word. First learn what a fixed-window predictor already does; then follow how the 2010 model carries history into a probability distribution."}</p>
      <dl class="stage-meta"><div><dt>Source</dt><dd>{view.canonicalVersion.label}</dd></div><div><dt>Your task</dt><dd>{view.objective.objective}</dd></div><div><dt>Success</dt><dd>{view.objective.successCriterion}</dd></div></dl>
    </header>
    <BengioPrerequisite interlude={prerequisite} mode={mode} />
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-lineage">
      <p class="kicker">Lineage · return to the state-to-word-model branch</p><h2 id="word-model-lineage">Carry state without importing a gated cell.</h2>
      <p>Mikolov names Elman’s simple recurrent architecture and addresses the fixed-window limitation of Bengio’s predictor. This chapter returns to the first branch: the 2010 model uses sigmoid hidden state, not the original LSTM, a forget gate, or peepholes. The later 2012 model will join the protected-cell branch to word prediction.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">An earlier word can influence the saved state; it is not retained as an individually retrievable word slot. The proposal removes an explicit fixed history length, but the conclusion limits the long context actually captured.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("elman-recurrent-architecture")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("fixed-window-motivation")} />
      <a class="claim-link mt-3" href={`/journeys/recurrent-memory/${mode}/elman-1988-1990/`}>Revisit Elman’s carried state</a>
    </section>

  </div>
  <aside class="stage-workbench stage-workbench--long" aria-label="Normalized word prediction and depth-one temporal credit"><WordPredictionLedger mode={mode} /></aside>
  <div class="stage-conclusion word-model-evidence">
    {expert && <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-equations">
      <p class="kicker">Source equations 1–6 · printed p. 1046</p><h2 id="word-model-equations">Read the dimensions before the plus sign.</h2>
      <div class="equation-panel mt-3" role="region" aria-label="Mikolov printed input equation" tabindex="0"><div set:html={input} /></div>
      <p>The source prints +, but its prose specifies concatenation: x has |V| + h entries, w is one-hot over word inputs, and s(t−1) has h entries. They are joined, not added elementwise. Replacing + with a concatenation operator is a notation clarification, not a different architecture.</p>
      <div class="equation-panel mt-3" role="region" aria-label="Mikolov hidden and output equations" tabindex="0"><div set:html={hidden} /><div set:html={output} /></div>
      <p>u ji weights input i into current state unit j; v kj weights state j into output k. The hidden update uses sigmoid, and the output uses softmax. Unlike Bengio’s score vector y, this y is already a probability distribution for w(t+1). No extra embedding lookup or LSTM mechanism is specified here.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("elman-recurrent-architecture")} />
      <div class="equation-panel mt-3" role="region" aria-label="Mikolov current-target error equation" tabindex="0"><div set:html={error} /></div>
      <p>Equation 6 is the cross-entropy output error convention; it is not a scalar loss. The static ledger’s beta example derives the one-token loss separately and makes no parameter-gradient claim.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">The paper initializes weights with zero-mean Gaussian noise of variance 0.1 and suggests a small state such as 0.1. Training starts at learning rate 0.1; validation log-likelihood governs continuation, halving, and stopping. Reported convergence is typically 10–20 epochs, not a guaranteed training budget.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("training-objective")} />
    </section>}
    <div class:list={["word-mode-blocks", { "word-mode-blocks--beginner": !expert }]}>{view.blocks.map((block) => <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby={block.id}><p class="kicker">{block.kind}</p><h2 id={block.id}>{block.heading}</h2><p>{block.body}</p>{block.evidence.map((evidence) => <EvidenceDetails evidence={evidence} />)}</section>)}</div>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-rare">
      <p class="kicker">Output boundary · equation 7 · pp. 1046–1047</p><h2 id="word-model-rare">A rare bucket is not a distinct predictor for every word.</h2>
      <p>Words occurring fewer times than a chosen training threshold become one rare token. Its predicted probability is divided equally among the merged word types. The allocation preserves total probability, but cannot distinguish those types within that context.</p>
      {expert && <><div class="equation-panel mt-3" role="region" aria-label="Mikolov rare-word probability equation" tabindex="0"><div set:html={rare} /></div><p>C rare counts merged word types, not their occurrences. Frequent types use their own y i. The threshold and hidden size change across reported models; neither is a control on the output-only teaching example.</p></>}
      <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("rare-word-probability")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-adaptation">
      <p class="kicker">Evaluation boundary · activation and parameters</p><h2 id="word-model-adaptation">Moving state is different from updating weights.</h2>
      <p>A static model changes state as each word arrives while its learned weights stay fixed. The dynamic variant also performs one-pass stochastic-gradient updates on test text, normally at learning rate 0.1. This is an evaluation procedure, not an extra memory gate.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">The paper compares adaptation with a cache, warns that recognition hypotheses contain errors, and later points to sentence order as a probable explanation for one reversal. A dynamic result depends on the test protocol.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("dynamic-adaptation")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block word-model-protocol" aria-labelledby="word-model-protocol">
      <p class="kicker">Static evidence · how to read the result tables</p><h2 id="word-model-protocol">Name the data, mixture, and metric.</h2>
      <p>WSJ92/93 results rescore 100-best speech-recognition lists. The oracle WER is 6.1% on development and 9.5% on evaluation. Up to 37 million NYT Gigaword words are available, but RNN training uses at most 6.4 million; perplexity is measured on 230,000 held-out words.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">KN5 means a modified Kneser–Ney 5-gram. RNN h/r names hidden size h and rare-word threshold r. Combined WSJ systems use 0.75 RNN probability + 0.25 KN5 probability. Three-RNN rows first combine neural models; they must not be read as one RNN. A KN5 baseline printed in the mixture column is still the baseline alone.</p>
      <p class="mt-3"><strong>Perplexity (PPL)</strong> evaluates word probability; <strong>word error rate (WER)</strong> evaluates recognition output. Lower is better within each matched condition, but the metrics are different. No control here joins datasets, model sizes, test-time learning, or the synthetic ledger into one experiment.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("wsj-evaluation-scope")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-scaling">
      <p class="kicker">Table 1 · WSJ development · p. 1047</p><h2 id="word-model-scaling">The scaling rows already include a count model.</h2>
      {expert ? <>
        <table class="trace-table word-result-table mt-3"><caption>Table 1 · held-out perplexity by training words</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Words · RNN h/r</th><th scope="col">KN5</th><th scope="col">KN5 + RNN</th></tr></thead><tbody>{scaling.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.data} · {row.model}</th><td>{row.knPpl}</td><td>{row.mixPpl}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
        <table class="trace-table word-result-table mt-4"><caption>Table 1 · development WER by training words</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Words · RNN h/r</th><th scope="col">KN5</th><th scope="col">KN5 + RNN</th></tr></thead><tbody>{scaling.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.data} · {row.model}</th><td>{row.knWer}</td><td>{row.mixWer}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
      </> : <p>At 6.4 million words, KN5 reports perplexity 221 and WER 13.5%; the mixture with RNN 250/5 reports 156 and 11.7%. This is a neural/count mixture, not the RNN alone. The paper also reports 200,000-word and 1-million-word conditions.</p>}
      <p class="mt-3">The largest row changes hidden size and rare-word threshold as well as training size. The table does not isolate a pure data-scaling effect.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("wsj-scaling-results")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-ensemble">
      <p class="kicker">Table 2 · 6.4 million words · p. 1047</p><h2 id="word-model-ensemble">The strongest displayed row has several ingredients.</h2>
      {expert ? <>
        <table class="trace-table word-result-table mt-3"><caption>Table 2 · held-out perplexity · RNN and KN5 mixture</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">RNN</th><th scope="col">RNN + KN5</th></tr></thead><tbody>{ensemble.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.model}</th><td>{row.rnnPpl}</td><td>{row.mixPpl}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
        <table class="trace-table word-result-table mt-4"><caption>Table 2 · development WER · RNN and KN5 mixture</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">RNN</th><th scope="col">RNN + KN5</th></tr></thead><tbody>{ensemble.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.model}</th><td>{row.rnnWer}</td><td>{row.mixWer}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
      </> : <p>The baseline has WER 13.5%. One static RNN 250/5 has 12.3%; adding KN5 gives 11.7%. The best displayed 11.1% combines three dynamically adapted RNNs and KN5, a 2.4-point change from baseline (about 17.8% relatively).</p>}
      <p class="mt-3">The dynamic RNN ensemble has perplexity 128 alone and 121 with KN5. The separate prose result 112 mixes static and dynamic RNNs with adaptation rate 0.3; its exact component weights/run composition are not fully specified. The nearly 50% headline aligns with 221→112 (about 49.3%), not the displayed 221→128 (42.1%) or 221→121 (45.2%).</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("wsj-ensemble-results")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-cross-data">
      <p class="kicker">Table 3 · unequal component data · p. 1047</p><h2 id="word-model-cross-data">Adaptation can lose on evaluation.</h2>
      {expert ? <table class="trace-table word-result-table mt-3"><caption>Table 3 · WSJ development and evaluation WER</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System · count/comparison data</th><th scope="col">DEV</th><th scope="col">EVAL</th></tr></thead><tbody>{crossData.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.model}</th><td>{row.dev}</td><td>{row.eval}{row.footnote && <span class="table-note-ref"> · note 4</span>}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table> : <p>The 37-million-word KN5 baseline reports evaluation WER 17.2%. Its static three-RNN mixture reports 15.5%; the dynamic mixture reports 16.3%. Those RNNs still train on only 6.4 million words.</p>}
      <p id="word-model-order-note" class="provenance-note mt-3"><strong>Footnote 4:</strong> the value is 16.3%, followed by note 4, not 16.34%. The authors tentatively blame nonconsecutive evaluation sentences for the dynamic reversal. This is a proposed explanation, not a controlled order ablation.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">The static mixture changes evaluation WER 17.2→15.5, about 9.9% relatively. The dynamic development change 12.2→10.7 is about 12.3%, but dynamic evaluation 17.2→16.3 is about 5.2%. The surrounding “around 12%” prose does not describe every Table 3 row.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("wsj-cross-data-results")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-rt05">
      <p class="kicker">Table 4 · NIST RT05 · pp. 1047–1048</p><h2 id="word-model-rt05">In-domain neural data joins a much larger backoff model.</h2>
      <p>The independent-headset RT05 condition uses AMI-system hypotheses and an acoustic model trained on about 115 hours. The RT05 count model uses more than 1.3 billion words; RT09 extends it with additional data and changed cutoffs. Each RNN uses 5.4 million in-domain words from meeting transcriptions and Switchboard.</p>
      {expert ? <table class="trace-table word-result-table mt-3"><caption>Table 4 · RT05 WER · static and dynamic evaluation</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">Static</th><th scope="col">Dynamic</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rt05.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.model}</th><td>{row.static}</td><td>{row.dynamic}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table> : <p class="mt-3">RT09’s baseline WER is 24.1%. The best 22.8% combines three dynamic RNNs with RT09, a 1.3-point change (about 5.4% relatively). One static RNN 1000/5 alone reports 24.2%; its mixture with RT09 reports 23.4%.</p>}
      <p class="mt-3">Different component data sizes remain part of the system result. The WSJ mixture weights are not asserted as a fully specified RT05 interpolation recipe.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("rt05-evaluation-results")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="word-model-cost">
      <p class="kicker">Cost boundary · reported timing</p><h2 id="word-model-cost">A quoted timing comparison is not a controlled speed test.</h2>
      <p>The Brown example uses about 800,000 words, 100 hidden units, and rare threshold five, reporting about six hours for the basic recurrent implementation. It quotes 113 days for Bengio’s basic implementation and 26 hours with importance sampling on a similar task. Hardware, processor count, code, and stopping rules are not controlled; BLAS optimization and several-week large-model training are also reported.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("training-cost-comparison")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block word-model-limits" aria-labelledby="word-model-limits">
      <p class="kicker">Conclusion · current-step credit and long-context limits</p><h2 id="word-model-limits">Available history is not learned long-context competence.</h2>
      <p>The main training uses temporal depth one and the authors call it non-optimal. Deeper BPTT appears as future work with preliminary toy evidence. The conclusion still says truly long context was not captured; cache information remains complementary even to dynamic models with BPTT.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">No confidence intervals, statistical tests, repeated seeds, controlled throughput, or end-to-end latency accompany these results. The paper’s improvement language does not establish statistical significance. One official Interspeech version supplies this chapter’s printed pp. 1045–1048; September 2010 is the known conference month, not a verified presentation day.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("truncated-bptt-boundary")} /><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("long-context-and-evidence-boundary")} />
      <a class="claim-link mt-3" href={`/reference/works/${view.work.id}/`}>Inspect the complete thirteen-claim work record</a>
    </section>
    <nav class="reading-block" aria-label="Recurrent journey progression"><p class="kicker">Next distinction · chapter queued</p><h2>Join gated cells to word prediction · 2012</h2><p>Sundermeyer’s later composite cell brings the protected-memory branch into word modeling. Its source-specific cell inventory and count-model mixture are the final planned chapter and smoothing return.</p><p class="mt-4"><a class="claim-link" href={view.previous!.href}>Revisit state inspection</a></p><p class="mt-3"><a class="claim-link" href={`/journeys/recurrent-memory/${mode}/`}>Return to the {mode} route map</a></p><div class="mt-4"><JourneyMap journey={journey} currentSlug={view.stage.slug} mode={mode} compact /></div></nav>
  </div>
</article>
